<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thuin</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thuin is a town in the province of Hainaut in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, on a rocky spur above the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sambre">Sambre</a>. For centuries it was a fortified town of the Prince-Bishopric of Liège, and its belfry is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. Some historians place nearby the stronghold of the Aduatuci, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> besieged in 57 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thuin.jpg" alt="Thuin" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean-Pol GRANDMONT · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thuin_JPG001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
